{
  "image_name": "4fc4a985eb2731c7675704bb7baa3af1.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how an oncolytic virus can turn a tumor cell into a source of immune stimulation and anti-cancer killing. After infecting a cancer cell, the virus replicates and generates viral proteins and genome, which induce cellular stress, including ER stress, genotoxic stress, and reactive oxygen species. This leads to viral oncolysis, releasing viral particles, tumor antigens, and danger signals into the surrounding environment.\n\nThese released factors include PAMPs, DAMPs, and cytokines such as type I interferons, TNFα, IFNγ, and IL-12. Together they activate antigen-presenting cells through receptors such as TLRs and promote antigen uptake and presentation on MHC molecules. The activated antigen-presenting cell then stimulates T cells through antigen presentation and costimulatory molecules like CD28 and CD40/CD40L.\n\nThe overall result is a coordinated innate and adaptive immune response. NK cells and CD8+ T cells receive cytokine signals and become cytotoxic, enabling them to attack tumor cells. CD4+ T cells appear to support this response, including through IL-2 production and helper interactions with antigen-presenting cells. Overall, the image shows that oncolytic virotherapy works not only by directly lysing cancer cells but also by converting tumor destruction into a broader anti-tumor immune response.",
